1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,566
Hello welcome to W3C

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,766
Have you ever switch your web content

3
00:00:04,766 --> 00:00:06,533
from one language to another

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,666
and just find out the text got broken

5
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:13,666
today I'm going to present W3C's article

6
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:15,333
Text size in translation

7
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,200
and gonna show you how text size

8
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:20,100
gonna affect the readability

9
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:23,400
and accessibility of your web content across languages

10
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,166
so importantly

11
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,100
Let's take China's biggest e-commerce website

12
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:31,733
Taobao as an example

13
00:00:32,366 --> 00:00:34,366
if you switch into English

14
00:00:34,733 --> 00:00:36,333
you'll find that text wrap

15
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:38,900
splits a single word into two lines

16
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:40,966
words get cut off mid sentence

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,333
and if you click into this

18
00:00:45,166 --> 00:00:48,500
there is also text run out of space

19
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:51,733
it's a small visual detail

20
00:00:51,933 --> 00:00:55,933
but it instantly makes your content harder to read

21
00:00:56,266 --> 00:00:59,866
and less trustworthy for international users

22
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:03,933
and it all comes down to internationalization issues

23
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,500
let's take a closer look

24
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:10,266
this is an example of a 9 word English sentence

25
00:01:10,266 --> 00:01:11,166
very simple

26
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:15,600
then we have German Chinese Japanese translations

27
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:18,266
ideas are the same

28
00:01:18,566 --> 00:01:21,333
but the length of the line is longer or shorter

29
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:24,666
very differently among both European and

30
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:26,000
Asian languages

31
00:01:27,033 --> 00:01:30,566
this is the everyday reality of multilingual design

32
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:33,500
what fits neatly in one language

33
00:01:33,566 --> 00:01:36,866
may just overlap or collapse in another language

34
00:01:37,833 --> 00:01:41,766
Flickr has a great table of the ratio of different

35
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:43,900
expansion rates across languages

36
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:46,533
what you're seeing is a comparison

37
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:48,133
of how the word 'Views'

38
00:01:48,133 --> 00:01:52,033
changes in length when translated into other languages

39
00:01:52,466 --> 00:01:54,100
English just five letters

40
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:55,966
But translations differ

41
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:57,733
some stay close to that length

42
00:01:57,733 --> 00:01:59,733
some double or even triple it

43
00:02:00,166 --> 00:02:04,033
300% expansion rate from English to Italian

44
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,333
is not surprising for small strings such as this

45
00:02:08,133 --> 00:02:09,966
and in Koreana and Chinese

46
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:13,433
the lens is similar but it needs more vertical space

47
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,100
according to IBM's Global Design Guidelines

48
00:02:17,466 --> 00:02:20,066
text usually expands when translated

49
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,966
but here's something interesting

50
00:02:21,966 --> 00:02:23,333
you can see in the table

51
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,933
the shorter the original text

52
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:28,800
the larger is the expansion is likely to be

53
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:32,500
so if we don't plan for that growth

54
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:35,066
we end up with broken interfaces

55
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:37,900
balance that overflow labels that get cut off

56
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:41,100
and layouts that simply don't work anymore

57
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,100
we also have other complicating factors

58
00:02:45,166 --> 00:02:47,700
that affect your tax expansion

59
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:49,533
on your web content page

60
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:50,600
firstly

61
00:02:51,266 --> 00:02:53,700
a number of languages such as Finnish

62
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:54,966
German and Dutch

63
00:02:54,966 --> 00:02:55,766
create single

64
00:02:55,766 --> 00:02:57,200
large words to replace

65
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,100
what is a sequence of smaller words

66
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:00,400
in other languages

67
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:04,666
for example the English 'input processing features'

68
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:07,300
may become very very long in German

69
00:03:07,333 --> 00:03:08,533
so in English

70
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:10,966
that phrase can easily wrap into two lines

71
00:03:10,966 --> 00:03:12,466
when space is tight

72
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,933
but the German version it might not wrap at all

73
00:03:15,966 --> 00:03:17,666
it just keeps going

74
00:03:17,666 --> 00:03:19,866
and that can easily break your layout

75
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:23,600
then there are languages like Chinese

76
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,066
Japanese and Korean

77
00:03:25,366 --> 00:03:28,866
their characters are more complex than Latin letters

78
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:32,400
each word takes up more horizontal space

79
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,766
for instance the English word desktop

80
00:03:36,033 --> 00:03:39,666
the Japanese one has only seven characters

81
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:42,500
but it looks much wider on screen

82
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,066
and vertical space matters too

83
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:49,500
some scripts such as Thai Arabic and the vernacular

84
00:03:49,766 --> 00:03:50,900
have taller characters

85
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:53,400
and require more space between lines

86
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:55,300
in this example

87
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:56,300
you can see that

88
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:58,266
the same text in English and Thai

89
00:03:58,266 --> 00:03:59,866
both take up two lines

90
00:04:00,066 --> 00:04:02,833
but the Thai version is noticeably taller

91
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,400
and this just doesn't not only happen with Thai

92
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:09,900
many scripts including Arabic Chinese

93
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:11,233
Japanese and Korean

94
00:04:11,366 --> 00:04:14,000
need also extra height for clarity

95
00:04:14,966 --> 00:04:17,966
finally it's about abbreviations

96
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:19,566
when designing interfaces

97
00:04:19,566 --> 00:04:22,200
we sometimes shorten words to make them fit

98
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,233
but this can cause problems in translation

99
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:28,900
some languages just don't use them at all

100
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:29,700
or

101
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:32,700
they cannot shorten words without changing the meaning

102
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:35,500
Arabic for instance

103
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,233
builds words using compact root patterns with

104
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:40,266
prefixes and suffixes

105
00:04:40,566 --> 00:04:43,700
so abbreviating can easily distort their meaning

106
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:48,566
in general the more flexible your layout is

107
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,400
and the more consideration you have

108
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:52,066
given to other languages

109
00:04:52,366 --> 00:04:55,033
the better your design will handle the translation

110
00:04:56,033 --> 00:05:00,133
W3C has other articles which go deeper into this topic

111
00:05:00,233 --> 00:05:04,433
including more detailed guidance for web and UI design

112
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,300
here are the links of this content

113
00:05:07,566 --> 00:05:10,200
and you can also find them below this video